demo> /help sym
NAME
  /sym - Symbol table: name -> address, or address -> name+offset.

SYNOPSIS
  /sym <addr|name>

DESCRIPTION
  Current symbols = 12 (demo.symbols.json)
  
  Symbols come from your linker map: /sym.import <map> converts it to
  <cfg>.symbols.json beside the config (a generated file, overwritten on
  re-import) and loads it.  The sidecar auto-loads whenever the config
  loads -- TUI, CLI and MCP alike.
  
  Address forms:
    0x1000         hex
    1000h          hex (assembly suffix)
    1000           DECIMAL -- bare hex is never guessed
    main           symbol name (exact, case-sensitive)
    main+0x10      symbol plus offset (also main-4)
    tick@adc.c     duplicate static, qualified by file (adc or src/adc.c too)
    name.<bit>     reserved for bit/slice/field access; an exact symbol
                   name wins, so count.12 is the symbol count.12
  
  Search forms (/sym.search): exact name, glob (*main*, Mon*), regex
  (^Mon), or a case-insensitive substring.
  
  Commands:
    /sym <addr|name>        - name -> address, or address -> name+offset
    /sym.import <map>       - convert a linker map and load it
    /sym.load {path}        - reload the sidecar, or load an explicit file
    /sym.unload             - clear the loaded table (file untouched)
    /sym.search <pattern>   - search names
    /sym.info               - file, source, counts by section, range

AVAILABLE
  TUI: yes   CLI: yes   MCP: yes

SUBCOMMANDS
  /sym.import    Convert a linker map to <cfg>.symbols.json and load it.
  /sym.info      Show the loaded symbol table: file, source, counts by section, address range.
  /sym.load      Load a symbol table (default: <cfg>.symbols.json, the file /sym.import writes).
  /sym.search    Search symbols by name: exact, glob (*main*), regex (^Mon), or substring.
  /sym.unload    Clear the loaded symbol table.
  [143/187] /help sym (5.12ms)
demo> /help mem
NAME
  /mem - Device memory: dump and write bytes, resolve names via /sym.

DESCRIPTION
  Reads and writes device memory as bytes.  The device implements the
  termapy MEM spec (see /help memory):
  
    MEM.R <addr> <len>   -> rows "<ADDR>: <XX XX ...>" then OK
    MEM.W <addr> <hex>   -> OK
    MEM.INFO             -> {"max_block": 64, "address_bits": 32, "endian": "le"} then OK
    any failure          -> ERR <reason>
  
  Addresses take every /sym form: 0x1000, 1000h, 1000 (decimal), gTemp,
  main+0x10, tick@adc.c.  Dump rows are annotated with the containing
  symbol when a table is loaded.  Block limit, address width and byte
  order come from the profile's "memory" block, else from MEM.INFO, else
  the defaults (64 / 32 / le); /mem.info shows which.
  
  Every write is audited in the session log (address, bytes before and
  after, origin).  Over MCP /mem.write is destructive: confirm=true.
  
  Commands:
    /mem.dump <addr> {len}   - hexdump len bytes (default 64; 32, 0x20 or 20h)
    /mem.write <addr> <hex>  - write hex bytes (1B00, 1B 00, 0x1B 0x00)
    /mem.info                - dialect, max_block, address_bits, endian

AVAILABLE
  TUI: yes   CLI: yes   MCP: yes

SUBCOMMANDS
  /mem.dump     Hexdump bytes at an address or symbol (default 64).
  /mem.info     Show how memory access is configured: dialect, block limit, address width, endian.
  /mem.write    Write hex bytes at an address or symbol (audited).
  [144/187] /help mem (2.79ms)
